# numfmt — real-world examples

## Convert bytes to human-readable format

```sh
numfmt --to=iec --suffix=B --padding=7 1048576
#  1.0MB

numfmt --to=iec 1048576      # 1.0M
```

## Convert human-readable back to bytes

```sh
numfmt --from=iec 1M         # 1048576
numfmt --from=iec 4G         # 4294967296
```

## Homelab: human-readable directory sizes

```sh
du -sb /srv/plex/* | numfmt --to=iec
ls -l | numfmt --field=5 --to=iec --invalid=ignore
```

## Thousands separators

```sh
numfmt --grouping 1234567    # 1,234,567 (locale dependent)
```

| Flag       | Meaning                                |
| ---------- | -------------------------------------- |
| --to=iec   | output as K/M/G (1024-based)           |
| --from=iec | parse K/M/G input into bytes           |
| --suffix   | append a suffix (e.g. B)               |
| --padding  | pad output to a width                  |
| --field    | which whitespace field to convert      |
| --grouping | thousands separators per locale        |
